(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-immunology-quiz"]={"title":"Blood & Immunology — Physiology Quiz","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"White blood cells defend against infection through which two broad strategies?","choices":["Phagocytosis of invaders and forming antibodies/sensitized lymphocytes","Delivering oxygen to the tissues and removing carbon dioxide for exhalation","Clotting blood at injury sites and repairing the damaged vessel wall","Buffering plasma pH and transporting hormones to their target organs"],"answer":0,"exp":"WBCs both directly ingest invaders by phagocytosis and generate antibodies/sensitized lymphocytes that neutralize specific invaders. (B) and (D) describe red-cell and plasma roles, and (C) is the job of platelets and clotting factors, not the defensive strategies of WBCs."},{"q":"Among the white blood cell types listed in the deck, which functions specifically to activate blood clotting?","choices":["Neutrophils","Eosinophils","Platelets","Lymphocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Platelets (from megakaryocytes) are grouped with the WBCs here and function specifically to trigger blood clotting. Neutrophils and eosinophils are phagocytic/effector granulocytes, and lymphocytes serve the immune system — none of these initiate clotting."},{"q":"How does the number of white blood cells compare with red blood cells in the blood?","choices":["WBCs are roughly equal in number to RBCs","WBCs are about 700-fold fewer than RBCs","WBCs outnumber RBCs about 10-fold","WBCs are about 10-fold fewer than RBCs"],"answer":1,"exp":"With ~7,000 WBC/µL versus ~5,000,000 RBC/µL, WBCs are roughly 700-fold fewer than red cells. The other options misstate this large disparity."},{"q":"Where do granulocytes and monocytes primarily develop, and what happens to most of them before they are needed?","choices":["In lymph nodes; they enter the circulation immediately after forming","In the thymus; they are then held in the spleen until needed","In bone marrow; most are stored there until needed peripherally","In the liver; they finish maturing within the peripheral tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Granulocytes and monocytes develop in the bone marrow, where about three times as many are stored as circulate, ready for release. (A), (B), and (D) misplace both the developmental site and the storage pool."},{"q":"Where do most lymphocytes develop?","choices":["Peripheral lymphoid organs such as the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes","Exclusively within the red bone marrow of the flat bones and vertebrae","In the liver sinusoids and portal tracts throughout adult life","Within the circulating blood plasma and the interstitial fluid spaces"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymphocytes develop mostly in peripheral lymphoid organs (thymus, spleen, tonsils, lymph nodes, Peyer's patches). (B) describes granulocyte/monocyte development, and (C) and (D) are not sites of lymphocyte development."},{"q":"How do neutrophils and monocytes differ in their readiness to fight infection?","choices":["Neutrophils are immature on release; monocytes are fully active the moment they leave the marrow","Both remain immature until they are activated within the bone marrow itself","Neither can act until it has travelled to a lymph node and been primed","Neutrophils are mature and act immediately; monocytes mature into macrophages in the tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Neutrophils are mature cells that respond at once, whereas monocytes are relatively immature and become effective only after maturing into tissue macrophages. (A) reverses this, and (B) and (C) are incorrect."},{"q":"After entering the tissues, monocytes become macrophages that typically...","choices":["Die within a few hours of arriving","Can survive and function for months or longer","Return to the bloodstream as neutrophils","Convert directly into circulating lymphocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tissue macrophages are long-lived, surviving months or longer. (A) understates their lifespan, and (C) and (D) describe conversions that do not occur — macrophages do not revert to neutrophils or become lymphocytes."},{"q":"Which trio of properties allows neutrophils and macrophages to reach a site of infection?","choices":["Diapedesis, ameboid motion, and chemotaxis","Agglutination, precipitation, and lysis","Opsonization, margination, and apoptosis","Phagocytosis, degranulation, and mitosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"These cells cross vessel walls (diapedesis), crawl through tissue (ameboid motion), and steer toward chemical signals (chemotaxis). (B) lists antibody actions, (C) mixes unrelated processes, and (D) lists effector/reproductive functions, not modes of locomotion."},{"q":"Compared with a neutrophil, an activated tissue macrophage can...","choices":["Ingest only a single bacterium before it dies off","No longer carry out any phagocytosis at all","Phagocytize far more particles — up to about 100 bacteria","Only ingest viral particles, never whole bacteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Activated macrophages are highly effective phagocytes, able to engulf up to ~100 bacteria and even large particles like damaged red cells. (A), (C), and (D) all understate or misstate their phagocytic capacity."},{"q":"What is chemotaxis, as it applies to neutrophils and macrophages?","choices":["Random drifting of the cell along with the bloodstream","Attachment of the cell to the endothelial vessel wall","Directed movement toward the source of a chemical signal","Ingestion and digestion of a foreign particle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chemotaxis is directed movement toward (or away from) a chemical gradient — e.g., toward bacterial toxins or complement products. (A) is undirected, (B) describes margination, and (D) describes phagocytosis."},{"q":"How do lymph nodes help contain an infection?","choices":["They produce replacement red blood cells to make up for losses","Macrophages lining the sinuses phagocytize particles, preventing spread","They hold neutrophils in reserve until the infection has resolved","They filter dissolved oxygen out of the lymph before it returns"],"answer":1,"exp":"Macrophages lining the nodal sinuses phagocytize foreign particles carried in the lymph, keeping them from disseminating through the body. (A), (C), and (D) are not lymph-node functions."},{"q":"What is the correct path of lymph through a lymph node?","choices":["Efferent lymphatics → nodal sinuses → hilus → afferent lymphatics","Blood capillaries → nodal sinuses → node → arterial blood","Afferent lymphatics → nodal sinuses → hilus → efferent lymphatics","Hilus → afferent lymphatics → nodal sinuses → venous blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lymph enters through afferent lymphatics, flows through the medullary sinuses, and exits at the hilus via efferent lymphatics toward venous blood. (A) reverses the flow, and (B) and (D) misroute it."},{"q":"What is the role of Kupffer cells in the liver sinusoids?","choices":["They synthesize the plasma clotting factors released into blood","They store the iron released from senescent red blood cells","They produce the bile used for digestion in the small intestine","They remove nearly all bacteria from portal blood in a single pass"],"answer":3,"exp":"Kupffer cells are fixed macrophages that clear ~99% of bacteria from portal blood on a single pass. (A) and (C) are hepatocyte functions, and (B) is not the Kupffer cell's defensive role."},{"q":"Which specialized macrophages handle inhaled particles and microorganisms?","choices":["Kupffer cells","Histiocytes","Alveolar macrophages","Splenic macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Alveolar macrophages digest or entrap particles and microbes reaching the lungs. Kupffer cells guard portal blood in the liver, histiocytes are skin/subcutaneous macrophages, and splenic macrophages screen circulating blood."},{"q":"The monocyte-macrophage (reticuloendothelial) system mainly functions to...","choices":["Remove dead or abnormal cells, tissues, and foreign substances","Transport oxygen from the lungs out to the peripheral tissues","Produce the antibodies that circulate in the plasma","Generate the clotting factors manufactured within the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"This distributed system of macrophages clears dead/abnormal cells and foreign material throughout the body. (B) is a red-cell role, (C) is a B-cell/plasma-cell role, and (D) is a hepatocyte role."},{"q":"Macrophages in the red pulp of the spleen are important for...","choices":["Producing erythropoietin to drive red cell output","Storing lymph fluid before it drains into the blood","Removing invading organisms, debris, and aged blood cells","Presenting antigen fragments exclusively to circulating B-cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Splenic red-pulp macrophages are richly exposed to circulating blood and remove organisms, debris, and senescent cells and platelets. (A), (C), and (D) misattribute their role."},{"q":"A distinctive feature of lymphocytes, compared with granulocytes, is that they...","choices":["Live for only a few hours once they reach the circulation","Cannot leave the bloodstream once they have entered it","Are unable to enter the lymph nodes at any stage","Continuously recirculate between blood and lymph and are long-lived"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lymphocytes continuously recirculate blood → lymph → nodes → blood and can live for weeks, months, or longer. (A) describes granulocytes, and (B) and (C) contradict lymphocyte recirculation."},{"q":"Why are the tonsils and adenoids positioned in the throat and pharynx?","choices":["To filter aged and damaged red cells out of the blood","To intercept antigens entering from the upper respiratory tract","To store fully mature T-cells before releasing them","To produce the lymph fluid that drains the head"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lymphoid tissue is placed strategically; tonsils and adenoids intercept antigens from the upper respiratory tract. (A), (C), and (D) are not their function."},{"q":"Compared with blood capillaries, lymph capillaries are...","choices":["Much less permeable to particles and large proteins","Completely impermeable to proteins of any size","Highly permeable, allowing particles to enter the lymph","Identical to them in permeability and structure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lymph capillaries are highly permeable relative to blood capillaries, so particles readily enter the lymph (where nodal macrophages can screen them). (A), (B), and (D) misstate this permeability."},{"q":"During inflammation, neutrophils first stick to the vessel wall (margination). What mediates this sticking step?","choices":["Perforin molecules binding directly to the endothelial surface","Adhesion molecules on the endothelium binding integrins on the neutrophil","Antibodies cross-linking neutrophils onto the vessel lining","Complement proteins lysing the underlying endothelial cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inflammatory cytokines induce endothelial adhesion molecules (selectins, ICAM-1) that bind complementary integrins on neutrophils, causing margination before diapedesis. (A), (C), and (D) describe unrelated processes."},{"q":"Which feature makes a particle MORE likely to be phagocytized?","choices":["A smooth surface","An intact protective protein coat","A negative charge that repels phagocytes","A rough surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rough surfaces favor phagocytosis, whereas the body's own smooth-surfaced, protein-coated structures resist it. So (A) and (B) describe features that resist phagocytosis, and (C) is not one of the three selective cues described."},{"q":"What does opsonization accomplish?","choices":["It marks a pathogen (with antibody and complement C3) for easier phagocytosis","It directly lyses the pathogen's outer membrane on contact","It prevents circulating antibodies from binding to the pathogen surface","It neutralizes the active site of a secreted bacterial toxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Opsonization tags a pathogen with antibody plus complement C3, attracting phagocytes and making ingestion far easier. (B) is complement-mediated lysis, (C) is the opposite of what happens, and (D) describes neutralization."},{"q":"How is an ingested microbe digested inside a phagocyte?","choices":["The phagosome fuses with a lysosome, exposing the microbe to digestive enzymes","The microbe is expelled from the cell and then lysed outside it","Antibodies stored inside the cell dissolve the ingested microbe","The cell nucleus engulfs the microbe and degrades it internally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The phagosome fuses with a lysosome to form a phagolysosome, where proteolytic enzymes (and lipases in macrophages) digest the microbe. (B), (C), and (D) do not describe intracellular digestion."},{"q":"Which set of changes characterizes acute inflammation?","choices":["Vasoconstriction, decreased capillary permeability, and leukocyte exclusion","Reduced local blood flow, suppressed immunity, and fewer leukocytes","Vasodilation, increased capillary permeability, and leukocyte accumulation","Increased red-cell production and a fall in the circulating white-cell count"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inflammation features vasodilation with increased blood flow, greater capillary permeability, and accumulation of granulocytes and monocytes. (A) is the opposite, and (C) and (D) misdescribe the response."},{"q":"How does fibrinogen leaking from damaged capillaries help contain an infection?","choices":["It clots in the tissue, walling off the area and limiting bacterial spread","It directly kills the bacteria by phagocytosing them in the tissue","It dissolves the cell walls of the invading bacteria","It attracts red blood cells to the site of the injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Leaked fibrinogen clots in the interstitium, minimizing fluid exchange and 'walling off' the area so bacteria spread less. (B), (C), and (D) are not fibrinogen's role."},{"q":"What provides the FIRST line of cellular defense at a new site of infection?","choices":["Neutrophils migrating out of the blood into the tissue","Increased bone marrow output of new white cells into blood","Circulating antibodies already present in the plasma","Tissue macrophages already present, which enlarge and mobilize"],"answer":3,"exp":"Resident tissue macrophages act first, enlarging and becoming mobile. Neutrophils are the second line (within ~1 hour), a second macrophage wave is third, and increased marrow output is fourth."},{"q":"As a later line of defense, how does the bone marrow respond to a sustained infection?","choices":["It shuts down white-cell production to conserve resources","It increases granulocyte and monocyte production many-fold","It releases mature lymphocytes within minutes","It converts red cells into white cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The marrow can raise neutrophil and monocyte output 20–50-fold and sustain it for months, with the first new cells released in 3–4 days. (A) is the opposite, and (C) and (D) are physiologically wrong."},{"q":"Eosinophils are especially important in defense against...","choices":["Intracellular viruses","Encapsulated bacteria","Parasites such as helminths (worms)","Fungal spores circulating in the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Eosinophils attach to parasites and release toxic substances (including major basic protein) that kill them. They are weak phagocytes, so (A), (B), and (D) are not their specialty."},{"q":"What triggers mast cells and basophils to degranulate?","choices":["Direct phagocytosis of a bacterium by the mast cell","Binding of complement C3 to the mast cell surface","Contact between the cell and a rough particle surface","Cross-linking of surface-bound IgE by its specific antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"When antigen cross-links IgE already bound to their surface receptors, mast cells and basophils degranulate, releasing histamine, heparin, and other mediators. (A), (B), and (C) do not trigger this release."},{"q":"When an antibody covers the toxic site of a toxin so it can no longer act, this is called...","choices":["Agglutination","Precipitation","Neutralization","Opsonization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Neutralization is the antibody covering the harmful sites of an antigen/toxin. Agglutination clumps particles, precipitation makes a soluble complex insoluble, and opsonization tags a pathogen for phagocytosis."},{"q":"Antibodies binding many bacteria together into a clump is an example of...","choices":["Agglutination","Neutralization","Lysis","Precipitation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Agglutination binds multiple particles bearing surface antigens into a clump. Neutralization masks toxic sites, lysis ruptures the target membrane, and precipitation drives a soluble antigen-antibody complex out of solution."},{"q":"What is the main role of the complement system?","choices":["To produce the specific antibodies directed against each antigen","To enhance antibody and phagocyte actions and attack pathogen membranes","To present processed antigen fragments to helper T-cells","To generate the memory cells used on future exposures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Complement 'complements' antibodies and phagocytes — promoting clearance, driving inflammation, and attacking pathogen membranes. (A) is a B-cell role, (C) is antigen presentation, and (D) is a memory-cell function."},{"q":"Which part of an antibody molecule binds to its specific antigen?","choices":["The constant portion of the heavy chains","The disulfide bonds linking the chains","The complement-binding region","The variable portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"The variable portion differs for each antibody and forms the antigen-binding site. The constant portion governs properties like diffusivity and complement binding, disulfide bonds hold the chains together, and the complement region is separate from antigen binding."},{"q":"How do cytotoxic (killer) T-cells destroy their targets?","choices":["By engulfing the target cell and digesting it through phagocytosis","By walling the target cell off inside a mesh of fibrin","By producing large amounts of antibody that circulates in plasma","By secreting perforins that punch holes and delivering cytotoxic substances"],"answer":3,"exp":"Killer T-cells bind a target bearing its antigen, secrete pore-forming perforins, and release cytotoxic substances directly into the cell. (A) is a phagocyte's method, (C) is humoral immunity, and (D) is inflammation's walling-off."},{"q":"Why are helper T-cells described as central to the immune response?","choices":["They directly lyse virus-infected cells using secreted perforins","Their cytokines regulate B-cells, cytotoxic T-cells, and macrophages","They are the only cells in the body that phagocytize bacteria","They form all of the antibodies used in humoral immunity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Helper T-cells secrete cytokines/lymphokines that coordinate B-cells, cytotoxic T-cells, and macrophages — the immune system is nearly paralyzed without them. (A) is the cytotoxic T-cell's job, (C) is a phagocyte role, and (D) is the plasma cell's job."},{"q":"How do T-cells recognize antigen, unlike B-cells?","choices":["They recognize antigen fragments presented on MHC molecules","They bind intact free antigen directly, without any processing","They detect antigen only once it has been agglutinated by antibody","They require complement proteins in order to bind any antigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"T-cells respond only to processed antigen fragments displayed on MHC molecules of antigen-presenting cells, whereas B-cells (and antibodies) recognize intact antigen. (C) and (D) are not requirements for T-cell recognition."},{"q":"Which statement best describes innate immunity?","choices":["It targets one specific antigen using antibodies tailored to it","It develops only after weeks of exposure to a particular pathogen","It relies on memory cells left behind by a prior infection","It resists organisms and toxins using non-specific, general defenses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Innate immunity uses non-specific, ready-made defenses (skin, gastric acid, neutrophils/macrophages, complement, NK cells). (A), (B), and (C) all describe acquired immunity, which is specific, slower to develop, and memory-based."},{"q":"A key contrast between acquired and innate immunity is that acquired immunity...","choices":["Is present and fully active from the moment of birth onward","Does not develop until the body is attacked and can take weeks to months","Provides no protection at all against bacterial toxins","Cannot distinguish one antigen from another in any way"],"answer":1,"exp":"Acquired immunity develops only after exposure, requiring weeks to months to mount a specific response. (A) describes innate defenses, and (C) and (D) contradict acquired immunity's specificity and potency."},{"q":"The two branches of acquired immunity are...","choices":["Humoral (B-cell/antibody) immunity and cell-mediated (T-cell) immunity","Phagocytosis by neutrophils and activation of the complement system","Local inflammation and the systemic fever response","Physical skin barriers and the acidity of gastric secretions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Acquired immunity comprises humoral immunity (circulating antibodies from B-cells) and cell-mediated immunity (activated T-cells). (B) and (D) are innate mechanisms, and (C) lists non-specific responses."},{"q":"Which cells provide humoral immunity, and how?","choices":["Cytotoxic T-cells, by lysing infected cells","Macrophages, by phagocytizing antigens","Natural killer cells, by non-specific killing","B-lymphocytes, by forming antibodies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Humoral ('circulating') immunity comes from B-lymphocytes that produce antibodies, while T-cells provide cell-mediated immunity. (A) is cell-mediated, and (B) and (C) are effector mechanisms not classed as humoral immunity."},{"q":"What is an antigen, and which part of it does the immune system specifically recognize?","choices":["A carbohydrate on the red cell surface; recognized by its overall charge","A protein or large polysaccharide; recognized at specific sites called epitopes","A small lipid molecule; recognized by its solubility in membranes","A trace metal ion; recognized directly by the complement system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Antigens are typically proteins or large polysaccharides (MW > 8,000) with recurring surface groups; the specific sites recognized are epitopes. The other options misidentify both the molecule and the recognized region."},{"q":"Where are future T-lymphocytes and B-lymphocytes 'preprocessed'?","choices":["T-cells in the thymus; B-cells in the bone marrow (and fetal liver)","Both of them within the thymus gland","Both of them within the red pulp of the spleen","T-cells in the bone marrow; B-cells in the thymus (and fetal liver)"],"answer":0,"exp":"T-cells are preprocessed in the thymus, while B-cells are preprocessed in the fetal liver and then the bone marrow. (B), (C), and (D) misassign one or both lineages."},{"q":"According to clonal selection, what happens when an antigen enters the body?","choices":["Every lymphocyte in the body responds to it equally and begins dividing","All circulating lymphocytes rearrange their receptors to match the antigen","It activates the pre-existing clone whose receptors are complementary, which then proliferates","It suppresses further lymphocyte division in order to conserve energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"An antigen selects the pre-existing clone bearing complementary receptors, which then multiplies and secretes matching antibodies. (A) and (B) contradict clonal specificity, and (D) is the opposite of what occurs."},{"q":"How can the body generate millions of distinct antibodies from only a few thousand relevant genes?","choices":["Each individual antibody is coded for by its own dedicated gene","Antibodies mutate at random following each separate infection","Complement proteins rearrange the antibodies already in the plasma","Hundreds of gene 'cassettes' (segments) recombine in many permutations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Diversity arises because hundreds of gene segments (cassettes) combine in vast numbers of permutations, yielding millions of specificities. (A) contradicts the segment model, and (B) and (C) are not the mechanism described."},{"q":"How does the body mainly avoid attacking its own tissues (immunologic tolerance)?","choices":["Self-reactive T- and B-cell clones are induced to undergo apoptosis and are deleted","Self-antigens are permanently hidden away from every immune cell in the body","Antibodies directed against self are neutralized by the complement system","Helper T-cells simply ignore every antigen that they encounter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tolerance is achieved largely by clonal deletion: clones that bind self-antigens with high affinity are driven into apoptosis (T-cells in the thymus, B-cells in the marrow). (B), (C), and (D) are not the primary mechanism."},{"q":"What is the proposed role of suppressor (regulatory) T-cells?","choices":["To amplify the immune response to every antigen that is encountered","To secrete the antibodies used during the humoral immune response","To present antigen fragments on MHC molecules to other cells","To help maintain tolerance to self-antigens and limit autoimmunity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Suppressor/regulatory T-cells are thought to restrain helper and cytotoxic T-cells, maintaining self-tolerance and preventing autoimmunity. (A) is the opposite effect, (C) is an APC role, and (D) is a plasma-cell function."},{"q":"Active immunity is produced by...","choices":["Infusing preformed antibodies taken from an already immune donor","Exposure to killed organisms, their products, or attenuated organisms","Blocking activation of the complement cascade","Surgically removing the thymus gland in infancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Active immunity develops when the body is exposed to killed organisms/toxoids or attenuated organisms and mounts its own response. (A) describes passive immunity, and (C) and (D) would impair, not create, immunity."},{"q":"How does passive immunity differ from active immunity?","choices":["It is generated by the person's own lymphocytes in the weeks after infection","It produces lifelong protection by laying down long-lived memory cells","It involves transferring preformed antibodies and is relatively short-lived","It relies on innate barriers such as intact skin and gastric acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Passive immunity comes from infused preformed antibodies (or activated cells) from an immune individual and lasts only ~2–3 weeks. (A) and (B) describe active immunity, and (D) describes innate immunity."},{"q":"Why is a second exposure to the same antigen met with a stronger, faster response?","choices":["The first exposure eliminated all lymphocytes, so replacements react faster","Memory B- and T-cells from the first exposure persist and respond rapidly","Complement permanently coats the antigen after the first exposure","The antigen becomes weaker over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Memory B- and T-cells generated during the first exposure remain and mount a rapid, amplified secondary response — the basis for booster immunizations. (A), (C), and (D) do not explain the secondary response."},{"q":"Which statement about the complement system best captures how it bridges innate and acquired immunity?","choices":["It is part of innate immunity, yet its classical pathway can be activated by antibodies from the adaptive system","It is part of acquired immunity and lays down memory cells for later exposures","It is adaptable and changes progressively over the course of a person's lifetime","It presents processed antigen fragments to T-cells on MHC molecules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Complement is a fixed, non-adaptive (innate) set of proteins, but antibodies from the adaptive system can trigger its cascade — linking the two arms. (B) and (C) wrongly call it adaptive/changeable, and (D) describes antigen presentation, not complement."}]};
